{"offset": 2600, "next": 2700, "data": [{"citingPaper": {"paperId": "4d0040b56712e51cda2dd9ad7299551de17a229c", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2305.10424", "DBLP": "conf/iclr/VedderPCKEJLRH24", "CorpusId": 262464439}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/4d0040b56712e51cda2dd9ad7299551de17a229c", "title": "ZeroFlow: Scalable Scene Flow via Distillation", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.10424, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2023-05-17", "authors": [{"authorId": "52173935", "name": "Kyle Vedder"}, {"authorId": "46191110", "name": "Neehar Peri"}, {"authorId": "3414229", "name": "Nathaniel Chodosh"}, {"authorId": "1500525828", "name": "Ishan Khatri"}, {"authorId": "144020269", "name": "Eric Eaton"}, {"authorId": "144348441", "name": "Dinesh Jayaraman"}, {"authorId": "46399556", "name": "Yang Liu"}, {"authorId": "1770537", "name": "D. Ramanan"}, {"authorId": "48966748", "name": "James Hays"}], "abstract": "Scene flow estimation is the task of describing the 3D motion field between temporally successive point clouds. State-of-the-art methods use strong priors and test-time optimization techniques, but require on the order of tens of seconds to process full-size point clouds, making them unusable as computer vision primitives for real-time applications such as open world object detection. Feedforward methods are considerably faster, running on the order of tens to hundreds of milliseconds for full-size point clouds, but require expensive human supervision. To address both limitations, we propose Scene Flow via Distillation, a simple, scalable distillation framework that uses a label-free optimization method to produce pseudo-labels to supervise a feedforward model. Our instantiation of this framework, ZeroFlow, achieves state-of-the-art performance on the Argoverse 2 Self-Supervised Scene Flow Challenge while using zero human labels by simply training on large-scale, diverse unlabeled data. At test-time, ZeroFlow is over 1000x faster than label-free state-of-the-art optimization-based methods on full-size point clouds (34 FPS vs 0.028 FPS) and over 1000x cheaper to train on unlabeled data compared to the cost of human annotation (\\$394 vs ~\\$750,000). To facilitate further research, we release our code, trained model weights, and high quality pseudo-labels for the Argoverse 2 and Waymo Open datasets at https://vedder.io/zeroflow.html"}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "ae783820a1487b964411cd0086324b89a3d64e0d", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2303.16563", "CorpusId": 265609805}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/ae783820a1487b964411cd0086324b89a3d64e0d", "title": "Skill Reinforcement Learning and Planning for Open-World Long-Horizon Tasks", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.16563, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2023-03-29", "authors": [{"authorId": "1429192914", "name": "Hao-Qi Yuan"}, {"authorId": "2269711204", "name": "Chi Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2157834018", "name": "Hong-Chen Wang"}, {"authorId": "2152182031", "name": "Fei-Yang Xie"}, {"authorId": "2139688739", "name": "Penglin Cai"}, {"authorId": "2269756326", "name": "Hao Dong"}, {"authorId": "2258676670", "name": "Zong-Qing Lu"}], "abstract": "We study building multi-task agents in open-world environments. Without human demonstrations, learning to accomplish long-horizon tasks in a large open-world environment with reinforcement learning (RL) is extremely inefficient. To tackle this challenge, we convert the multi-task learning problem into learning basic skills and planning over the skills. Using the popular open-world game Minecraft as the testbed, we propose three types of fine-grained basic skills, and use RL with intrinsic rewards to acquire skills. A novel Finding-skill that performs exploration to find diverse items provides better initialization for other skills, improving the sample efficiency for skill learning. In skill planning, we leverage the prior knowledge in Large Language Models to find the relationships between skills and build a skill graph. When the agent is solving a task, our skill search algorithm walks on the skill graph and generates the proper skill plans for the agent. In experiments, our method accomplishes 40 diverse Minecraft tasks, where many tasks require sequentially executing for more than 10 skills. Our method outperforms baselines by a large margin and is the most sample-efficient demonstration-free RL method to solve Minecraft Tech Tree tasks. The project's website and code can be found at https://sites.google.com/view/plan4mc."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "b4c176029ddf95863b453096bbd43ae9651a2424", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2303.10571", "DBLP": "conf/eccv/JiangYLDL24", "DOI": "10.1007/978-3-031-73113-6_1", "CorpusId": 257632482}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/b4c176029ddf95863b453096bbd43ae9651a2424", "title": "Reinforcement Learning Friendly Vision-Language Model for Minecraft", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2303.10571", "status": "GREEN", "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.10571, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2023-03-19", "authors": [{"authorId": "2152632624", "name": "Hao-Bin Jiang"}, {"authorId": "95759422", "name": "Junpeng Yue"}, {"authorId": "2199828096", "name": "Hao Luo"}, {"authorId": "1742314398", "name": "Zi-Luo Ding"}, {"authorId": "2258676670", "name": "Zong-Qing Lu"}], "abstract": "One of the essential missions in the AI research community is to build an autonomous embodied agent that can achieve high-level performance across a wide spectrum of tasks. However, acquiring or manually designing rewards for all open-ended tasks is unrealistic. In this paper, we propose a novel cross-modal contrastive learning framework architecture, CLIP4MC, aiming to learn a reinforcement learning (RL) friendly vision-language model (VLM) that serves as an intrinsic reward function for open-ended tasks. Simply utilizing the similarity between the video snippet and the language prompt is not RL-friendly since standard VLMs may only capture the similarity at a coarse level. To achieve RL-friendliness, we incorporate the task completion degree into the VLM training objective, as this information can assist agents in distinguishing the importance between different states. Moreover, we provide neat YouTube datasets based on the large-scale YouTube database provided by MineDojo. Specifically, two rounds of filtering operations guarantee that the dataset covers enough essential information and that the video-text pair is highly correlated. Empirically, we demonstrate that the proposed method achieves better performance on RL tasks compared with baselines. The code and datasets are available at https://github.com/PKU-RL/CLIP4MC."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "f2d952a183dfb0a1e031b8a3f535d9f8423d7a6e", "externalIds": {"DBLP": "journals/corr/abs-2301-04104", "ArXiv": "2301.04104", "DOI": "10.48550/arXiv.2301.04104", "CorpusId": 255569874}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/f2d952a183dfb0a1e031b8a3f535d9f8423d7a6e", "title": "Mastering Diverse Domains through World Models", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "http://arxiv.org/pdf/2301.04104", "status": "GREEN", "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2301.04104, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science", "Mathematics"], "publicationDate": "2023-01-10", "authors": [{"authorId": "35006479", "name": "Danijar Hafner"}, {"authorId": "31143488", "name": "J. Pa\u0161ukonis"}, {"authorId": "2503659", "name": "Jimmy Ba"}, {"authorId": "2542999", "name": "T. Lillicrap"}], "abstract": "Developing a general algorithm that learns to solve tasks across a wide range of applications has been a fundamental challenge in artificial intelligence. Although current reinforcement learning algorithms can be readily applied to tasks similar to what they have been developed for, configuring them for new application domains requires significant human expertise and experimentation. We present DreamerV3, a general algorithm that outperforms specialized methods across over 150 diverse tasks, with a single configuration. Dreamer learns a model of the environment and improves its behavior by imagining future scenarios. Robustness techniques based on normalization, balancing, and transformations enable stable learning across domains. Applied out of the box, Dreamer is the first algorithm to collect diamonds in Minecraft from scratch without human data or curricula. This achievement has been posed as a significant challenge in artificial intelligence that requires exploring farsighted strategies from pixels and sparse rewards in an open world. Our work allows solving challenging control problems without extensive experimentation, making reinforcement learning broadly applicable."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "638b5c76d96e32f54475a8327a9c68e0167156a9", "externalIds": {"DBLP": "journals/corr/abs-2301-03044", "ArXiv": "2301.03044", "DOI": "10.48550/arXiv.2301.03044", "CorpusId": 255546304}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/638b5c76d96e32f54475a8327a9c68e0167156a9", "title": "A Survey on Transformers in Reinforcement Learning", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2301.03044", "status": "GREEN", "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2301.03044, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2023-01-08", "authors": [{"authorId": "2159213989", "name": "Wenzhe Li"}, {"authorId": "2199828096", "name": "Hao Luo"}, {"authorId": "41123614", "name": "Zi-Chuan Lin"}, {"authorId": "2111387140", "name": "Chong-Jie Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2258676670", "name": "Zong-Qing Lu"}, {"authorId": "2055648566", "name": "De-Heng Ye"}], "abstract": "Transformer has been considered the dominating neural architecture in NLP and CV, mostly under supervised settings. Recently, a similar surge of using Transformers has appeared in the domain of reinforcement learning (RL), but it is faced with unique design choices and challenges brought by the nature of RL. However, the evolution of Transformers in RL has not yet been well unraveled. In this paper, we seek to systematically review motivations and progress on using Transformers in RL, provide a taxonomy on existing works, discuss each sub-field, and summarize future prospects."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "25425e299101b13ec2872417a14f961f4f8aa18e", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2210.03094", "DBLP": "journals/corr/abs-2210-03094", "DOI": "10.48550/arXiv.2210.03094", "CorpusId": 252735175}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/25425e299101b13ec2872417a14f961f4f8aa18e", "title": "VIMA: General Robot Manipulation with Multimodal Prompts", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "http://arxiv.org/pdf/2210.03094", "status": "GREEN", "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.03094, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2022-10-06", "authors": [{"authorId": "2171112793", "name": "Yunfan Jiang"}, {"authorId": "2315452357", "name": "Agrim Gupta"}, {"authorId": "5630943", "name": "Zichen Zhang"}, {"authorId": "96374437", "name": "Guanzhi Wang"}, {"authorId": "1768148923", "name": "Yongqiang Dou"}, {"authorId": "2187067176", "name": "Yan-Jun Chen"}, {"authorId": "48004138", "name": "Li Fei-Fei"}, {"authorId": "47627049", "name": "Anima Anandkumar"}, {"authorId": "2117748", "name": "Yuke Zhu"}, {"authorId": "3275727", "name": "Linxi (Jim) Fan"}], "abstract": "Prompt-based learning has emerged as a successful paradigm in natural language processing, where a single general-purpose language model can be instructed to perform any task specified by input prompts. Yet task specification in robotics comes in various forms, such as imitating one-shot demonstrations, following language instructions, and reaching visual goals. They are often considered different tasks and tackled by specialized models. We show that a wide spectrum of robot manipulation tasks can be expressed with multimodal prompts, interleaving textual and visual tokens. Accordingly, we develop a new simulation benchmark that consists of thousands of procedurally-generated tabletop tasks with multimodal prompts, 600K+ expert trajectories for imitation learning, and a four-level evaluation protocol for systematic generalization. We design a transformer-based robot agent, VIMA, that processes these prompts and outputs motor actions autoregressively. VIMA features a recipe that achieves strong model scalability and data efficiency. It outperforms alternative designs in the hardest zero-shot generalization setting by up to $2.9\\times$ task success rate given the same training data. With $10\\times$ less training data, VIMA still performs $2.7\\times$ better than the best competing variant. Code and video demos are available at https://vimalabs.github.io/"}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "fb877ede091abf3b00933cd0ca069e71bc2949e3", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2201.05000", "DBLP": "journals/corr/abs-2201-05000", "DOI": "10.48550/arXiv.2201.05000", "CorpusId": 245906433}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/fb877ede091abf3b00933cd0ca069e71bc2949e3", "title": "Automated Reinforcement Learning: An Overview", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "http://arxiv.org/pdf/2201.05000", "status": "GREEN", "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2201.05000, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2022-01-13", "authors": [{"authorId": "9323218", "name": "Reza Refaei Afshar"}, {"authorId": "1717534", "name": "J. Vanschoren"}, {"authorId": "1678244", "name": "U. Kaymak"}, {"authorId": "2349675131", "name": "Rui Zhang"}, {"authorId": "31274290", "name": "Yao-Xin Wu"}, {"authorId": "2287786736", "name": "Wen Song"}, {"authorId": "2297344146", "name": "Ying-Qian Zhang"}], "abstract": "Reinforcement Learning and, recently, Deep Reinforcement Learning are popular methods for solving sequential decision-making problems modeled as Markov Decision Processes. RL modeling of a problem and selecting algorithms and hyper-parameters require careful consideration, as different configurations may entail completely different performances. These considerations are mainly the task of RL experts; however, RL is progressively becoming popular in other fields, such as combinatorial optimization, where researchers and system designers are not necessarily RL experts. Besides, many modeling decisions are typically made manually, such as defining state and action space, size of batches, batch update frequency, and time steps. For these reasons, automating different components of RL is of great importance, and it has attracted much attention in recent years. Automated RL provides a framework in which different components of RL, including MDP modeling, algorithm selection, and hyper-parameter optimization, are modeled and defined automatically. In this article, we present the literature on automated RL (AutoRL), including the recent large language model (LLM) based techniques. We also discuss the recent work on techniques that are not presently tailored for automated RL but hold promise for future integration into AutoRL. Furthermore, we discuss the challenges, open questions, and research directions in AutoRL."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "740ac8c0062e49cc22be82368ac73314db2fb0d6", "externalIds": {"DBLP": "journals/corr/abs-2608-29596", "DOI": "10.48550/arXiv.2608.29596", "CorpusId": 295411078}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/740ac8c0062e49cc22be82368ac73314db2fb0d6", "title": "Towards a Systems Foundation for Agentic Skills: Architecture, Lifecycle, and Security", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: The following paper fields have been elided by the publisher: {'abstract'}. Paper or abstract available at https://api.unpaywall.org/v2/10.48550/arXiv.2608.29596?email=<INSERT_YOUR_EMAIL> or https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2608.29596, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": null, "authors": [{"authorId": "1477287294", "name": "Sanket Badhe"}, {"authorId": "2054171865", "name": "D. Shah"}, {"authorId": "2362921860", "name": "Priyanka Tiwari"}, {"authorId": "2407202073", "name": "Nehal Kathrotia"}], "abstract": null}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "e07558104c01693984964a3c8dce5aa9b2935d41", "externalIds": {"DBLP": "journals/corr/abs-2601-21545", "DOI": "10.48550/arXiv.2601.21545", "CorpusId": 285140512}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/e07558104c01693984964a3c8dce5aa9b2935d41", "title": "ShardMemo: Masked MoE Routing for Sharded Agentic LLM Memory", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: The following paper fields have been elided by the publisher: {'abstract'}. Paper or abstract available at https://api.unpaywall.org/v2/10.48550/arXiv.2601.21545?email=<INSERT_YOUR_EMAIL> or https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2601.21545, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": null, "authors": [{"authorId": "2366209057", "name": "Yang Zhao"}, {"authorId": "2366019512", "name": "Chengxiao Dai"}, {"authorId": "2381986590", "name": "Yue Xiu"}, {"authorId": "49155637", "name": "M. Kou"}, {"authorId": "2461526296", "name": "Yu-Liang Zheng"}, {"authorId": "2362086753", "name": "D. Niyato"}], "abstract": null}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "738814d7613e809671cf20fa8b539f098c546bcc", "externalIds": {"DBLP": "journals/access/RheeZMTY26", "DOI": "10.1109/ACCESS.2026.3717594", "CorpusId": 290668562}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/738814d7613e809671cf20fa8b539f098c546bcc", "title": "Enhancing Web Search Agents With Self-Play Contrastive Fine-Tuning", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "https://doi.org/10.1109/access.2026.3717594", "status": "GOLD", "license": "CCBY", "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://api.unpaywall.org/v2/10.1109/ACCESS.2026.3717594?email=<INSERT_YOUR_EMAIL> or https://doi.org/10.1109/ACCESS.2026.3717594, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": null, "authors": [{"authorId": "2443179312", "name": "Minjae Rhee"}, {"authorId": "2400502922", "name": "Ji-Tong Zou"}, {"authorId": "2450079781", "name": "Tianjun Mo"}, {"authorId": "2064884444", "name": "Dai Teng"}, {"authorId": "2254849817", "name": "Jie-Si Yang"}], "abstract": "Large language model (LLM)-based web search agents have demonstrated remarkable capabilities in autonomous information retrieval and multi-step reasoning. However, their robustness under real-world distribution shifts, such as evolving webpage structures, dynamic content layouts, and unseen task environments, remains a critical open challenge. Existing approaches predominantly rely on static supervised fine-tuning with human-annotated trajectories, which not only incurs substantial labeling cost but also lacks the adaptive capacity to handle the inherent stochasticity of live web environments. In this paper, we propose TAPE (Trajectory Alignment and exPerience pool Evolution), a novel self-evolving fine-tuning framework designed to enhance the generalization and robustness of web search agents without requiring large-scale human annotation. TAPE introduces a dual-stream experience pool that continuously accumulates both successful and failed agent trajectories during self-play execution. A trajectory alignment module maps heterogeneous execution paths into a unified semantic space, enabling contrastive learning to identify failure-inducing operations and reward generalizable search strategies. The framework further incorporates an adaptive pool evolution mechanism that filters, reweights, and distills experiences to prevent knowledge stagnation and distributional collapse. Extensive experiments on the GAIA benchmark and BrowseComp dataset demonstrate that TAPE consistently outperforms strong baselines across all three difficulty levels, achieving up to 6.2% absolute improvement in task success rate on GAIA Level-2 tasks (a 134.8% relative gain over the SFT-Only baseline on the GAIA validation split) and 1.3% absolute (217% relative) improvement on BrowseComp hard-tier queries, while exhibiting significantly greater resilience to webpage structure perturbations. These improvements are consistent across three open base models spanning two model families and the 7B\u201314B scale range. Our analysis further suggests that the contrastive self-play signal can serve as a useful partial surrogate for human preference labeling in agent trajectory optimization, substantially reducing, though not eliminating, reliance on human-annotated trajectories."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "be76dc326c5854608d7a2f6ac23a55dcb42590ac", "externalIds": {"DBLP": "journals/corr/abs-2601-22758", "DOI": "10.48550/arXiv.2601.22758", "CorpusId": 285241715}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/be76dc326c5854608d7a2f6ac23a55dcb42590ac", "title": "AutoRefine: From Trajectories to Reusable Expertise for Continual LLM Agent Refinement", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: The following paper fields have been elided by the publisher: {'abstract'}. Paper or abstract available at https://api.unpaywall.org/v2/10.48550/arXiv.2601.22758?email=<INSERT_YOUR_EMAIL> or https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2601.22758, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": null, "authors": [{"authorId": "2375321390", "name": "Li-Bin Qiu"}, {"authorId": "2375133649", "name": "Zhi-Rong Gao"}, {"authorId": "2374970451", "name": "Jun-Fu Chen"}, {"authorId": "2376094175", "name": "Yu-Hang Ye"}, {"authorId": "2374998973", "name": "Wei-Zhi Huang"}, {"authorId": "2375969847", "name": "Xiao-Bo Xue"}, {"authorId": "2374968083", "name": "Wen-Kai Qiu"}, {"authorId": "2283546526", "name": "Shuo Tang"}], "abstract": null}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "b86a246847e0d00372c606dd06c870f22dd1e8c9", "externalIds": {"DBLP": "journals/access/JiAEQ26", "DOI": "10.1109/ACCESS.2026.3654948", "CorpusId": 284837979}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/b86a246847e0d00372c606dd06c870f22dd1e8c9", "title": "CodeGen-3D: A Benchmark for Evaluating LLMs in Zero-Shot and Iterative 3D Modeling in Blender", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "https://doi.org/10.1109/access.2026.3654948", "status": "GOLD", "license": "CCBY", "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://api.unpaywall.org/v2/10.1109/ACCESS.2026.3654948?email=<INSERT_YOUR_EMAIL> or https://doi.org/10.1109/ACCESS.2026.3654948, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": null, "authors": [{"authorId": "91946363", "name": "Haobo Ji"}, {"authorId": "2409297733", "name": "K. Aditya"}, {"authorId": "145713101", "name": "Sebastian Escalante"}, {"authorId": "2405839543", "name": "Yun-Jian Qiu"}], "abstract": "Large Language Models (LLMs) that generate executable code are opening a new path to text-to-3D by converting natural language prompts into scripts for modeling software like Blender. However, there is a lack of evaluation for such methods currently. Existing benchmarks focus on direct mesh synthesis and overlook critical factors like code execution reliability and human-aligned quality. To address this gap, we introduce CodeGen-3D, a comprehensive benchmark and protocol for assessing 3D modeling via code generation. The benchmark consists of 100 diverse prompts derived from Objaverse with rich captions (Cap3D, DiffuRank, and Llama-based), a standardized Blender execution and six-view rendering pipeline, and a three-part evaluation protocol that assesses: 1) error rate (compilation and runtime failures), 2) multi-view CLIP alignment (average and max) for semantic similarity, and 3) a VLM-as-a-Judge pairwise preference task (using GPT-4o) for perceptual quality and prompt adherence. We report results for eight general-purpose LLMs, a specialized BlenderLLM, and an iterative self-correcting agent. Our analysis shows that specialized and iterative systems are substantially more reliable (3\u20134% failure rate vs. 21\u201392% for general LLMs) and obtain the highest GPT-4o preference rates (e.g., 33.3% win rate for the iterative agent), even when CLIP scores are similar. This gap between CLIP-based alignment and judge-based preferences underscores the need for feedback-driven, multi-signal evaluation, suggesting that practical text-to-3D systems should combine strong general LLMs with domain specialization or inexpensive self-refinement loops, rather than relying on single-pass code generation. CodeGen-3D establishes a practical, reproducible benchmark and offers strong baselines for future research at the intersection of LLMs, code generation, and 3D modeling."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "43692c8dc09ad81e5fb787adba5a8e0686f3cd9c", "externalIds": {"DBLP": "journals/corr/abs-2602-01664", "DOI": "10.48550/arXiv.2602.01664", "CorpusId": 285270176}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/43692c8dc09ad81e5fb787adba5a8e0686f3cd9c", "title": "FlowSteer: Interactive Agentic Workflow Orchestration via End-to-End Reinforcement Learning", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: The following paper fields have been elided by the publisher: {'abstract'}. Paper or abstract available at https://api.unpaywall.org/v2/10.48550/arXiv.2602.01664?email=<INSERT_YOUR_EMAIL> or https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2602.01664, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": null, "authors": [{"authorId": "2358581757", "name": "Ming-Da Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2384447799", "name": "Hao-Ran Luo"}, {"authorId": "2189825927", "name": "Tiesunlong Shen"}, {"authorId": "2316927371", "name": "Qi-Ka Lin"}, {"authorId": "2293581718", "name": "Xiao-Ying Tang"}, {"authorId": "2404466072", "name": "Rui Mao"}, {"authorId": "2334969437", "name": "Erik Cambria"}], "abstract": null}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "d5215b420cd0fdb90ecee6d3c619939254a9b674", "externalIds": {"DBLP": "conf/iclr/ZhouZLZCLZCFLSY26", "CorpusId": 285458960}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/d5215b420cd0fdb90ecee6d3c619939254a9b674", "title": "Virtual Community: An Open World for Humans, Robots, and Society", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": null, "authors": [{"authorId": "2107604346", "name": "Qinhong Zhou"}, {"authorId": "2332592758", "name": "Hong-Xin Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2319201696", "name": "Xiang-Ye Lin"}, {"authorId": "2163066286", "name": "Zheyuan Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2284701608", "name": "Yu-Tian Chen"}, {"authorId": "2265686380", "name": "Wen-Jun Liu"}, {"authorId": "2371083612", "name": "Zun-Zhe Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2261356954", "name": "Sun-Li Chen"}, {"authorId": "2331457795", "name": "Li-Xing Fang"}, {"authorId": "2296785330", "name": "Qiushi Lyu"}, {"authorId": "2257378355", "name": "Xinyu Sun"}, {"authorId": "2291198667", "name": "Jin-Cheng Yang"}, {"authorId": "2296941991", "name": "Ze-Yuan Wang"}, {"authorId": "2376329921", "name": "Bao-Chi Dang"}, {"authorId": "2294511957", "name": "Zhe-Huan Chen"}, {"authorId": "2376330573", "name": "Daksha Ladia"}, {"authorId": "2525202601", "name": "Quang Dang"}, {"authorId": "2298930379", "name": "Jia-Geng Liu"}, {"authorId": "2329100523", "name": "Chuang Gan"}], "abstract": null}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "08a23f2235d6a111ecf72dc28c6bce375e4950c8", "externalIds": {"DBLP": "journals/corr/abs-2602-22809", "DOI": "10.48550/arXiv.2602.22809", "CorpusId": 286082495}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/08a23f2235d6a111ecf72dc28c6bce375e4950c8", "title": "PhotoAgent: Agentic Photo Editing with Exploratory Visual Aesthetic Planning", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: The following paper fields have been elided by the publisher: {'abstract'}. Paper or abstract available at https://api.unpaywall.org/v2/10.48550/arXiv.2602.22809?email=<INSERT_YOUR_EMAIL> or https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2602.22809, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": null, "authors": [{"authorId": "2304446112", "name": "Ming-De Yao"}, {"authorId": "2274105323", "name": "Zhi-Yuan You"}, {"authorId": "2351803791", "name": "King-Man Tam"}, {"authorId": "2352776797", "name": "Menglu Wang"}, {"authorId": "2274105863", "name": "Tian-Fan Xue"}], "abstract": null}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "6a297a5e548195d3bc7d9f8075d4cb595b8b1e35", "externalIds": {"DBLP": "journals/corr/abs-2602-05073", "DOI": "10.48550/arXiv.2602.05073", "CorpusId": 286675327}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/6a297a5e548195d3bc7d9f8075d4cb595b8b1e35", "title": "Towards Reducible Uncertainty Modeling for Reliable Large Language Model Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: The following paper fields have been elided by the publisher: {'abstract'}. Paper or abstract available at https://api.unpaywall.org/v2/10.48550/arXiv.2602.05073?email=<INSERT_YOUR_EMAIL> or https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2602.05073, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": null, "authors": [{"authorId": "2152046120", "name": "Changdae Oh"}, {"authorId": "2346375454", "name": "Seongheon Park"}, {"authorId": "2175557537", "name": "To Eun Kim"}, {"authorId": "2259858493", "name": "Jia-Tong Li"}, {"authorId": "2326015753", "name": "Wen-Di Li"}, {"authorId": "2055073319", "name": "Samuel Yeh"}, {"authorId": "2246815275", "name": "Xuefeng Du"}, {"authorId": "2254218321", "name": "Hamed Hassani"}, {"authorId": "1698286", "name": "P. Bogdan"}, {"authorId": "2374189943", "name": "D. Song"}, {"authorId": "2385968241", "name": "Sharon Li"}], "abstract": null}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "69924651b210c5ff9d9e8c36476fdbd2002a39b7", "externalIds": {"DBLP": "conf/icaart/GalvanDCKSSKC26", "DOI": "10.5220/0014240600004052", "CorpusId": 286537678}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/69924651b210c5ff9d9e8c36476fdbd2002a39b7", "title": "A Multi-Agent LLM Framework with RAG for Shipbuilding Engineering Documents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "https://doi.org/10.5220/0014240600004052", "status": "HYBRID", "license": "CCBYNCND", "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://api.unpaywall.org/v2/10.5220/0014240600004052?email=<INSERT_YOUR_EMAIL> or https://doi.org/10.5220/0014240600004052, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": null, "authors": [{"authorId": "104105950", "name": "B. Galvan"}, {"authorId": "2142825900", "name": "G. Drakoulas"}, {"authorId": "1751646212", "name": "Bram Cals"}, {"authorId": "1801773", "name": "Tayfun Kucukyilmaz"}, {"authorId": "2379195390", "name": "Matthijs Schakel"}, {"authorId": "2457823645", "name": "Dina Semchenko"}, {"authorId": "96502925", "name": "Juri Kuzjatkin"}, {"authorId": "1617519903", "name": "G. Cabral"}], "abstract": ": In many industries, compliance relies on interpreting complex rulebooks that contain dense prose, technical tables, and engineering equations. In the maritime sector, these standards are considered critical to ensure that vessels meet regulatory standards. Yet, using manual labor for information extraction is time-consuming and inefficient. The development of Large Language Models and Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) systems offers a promising solution for the automation of such tasks. However, naive RAG systems often fail to combine varied data cohesively. To address that, this study proposes a novel multi-agent RAG architecture for maritime engineering standards, decomposing the complex processes and delegating sub-tasks to specialized agents. The system employs a five-agent pipeline: i) chapter-suggester, that suggests relevant chapters from manuals ii) main retriever, which is responsible for retrieving large pieces of information through a vector database, iii) prompt-generator, which generates prompts for missing information, iv) organizer, which organizes the retrieved information, and v) response-giver, which generates a final response. Results showed that different LLMs demonstrated varying performance in extracting engineering equations from shipbuilding manuals, with some achieving strong accuracy. For engineering computations, the multi-agent system showed moderate accuracy on simpler tasks but substantial errors on complex calculations, indicating the need for validation and hybrid approaches."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "46dea637b1ebce7f2b1ceb017f7f967861eca258", "externalIds": {"DBLP": "journals/access/RamamurthyKA26", "DOI": "10.1109/ACCESS.2026.3686019", "CorpusId": 287676551}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/46dea637b1ebce7f2b1ceb017f7f967861eca258", "title": "EvoGraphCoder: An Evolutionary Graph-Reasoning Framework for Self-Adaptive Software Engineering", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "https://doi.org/10.1109/access.2026.3686019", "status": "GOLD", "license": "CCBY", "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://api.unpaywall.org/v2/10.1109/ACCESS.2026.3686019?email=<INSERT_YOUR_EMAIL> or https://doi.org/10.1109/ACCESS.2026.3686019, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": null, "authors": [{"authorId": "50616595", "name": "K. Ramamurthy"}, {"authorId": "2379849011", "name": "Rahul Kumar Konduru"}, {"authorId": "2389942194", "name": "Nurmyrat Amanmadov"}], "abstract": "Modern software evolves rapidly, accumulates architectural debt, and develops cross-module dependencies that complicate reliable maintenance. We present EvoGraphCoder, an evolutionary graph-reasoning framework for self-adaptive software engineering. EvoGraphCoder represents source code, tests, commit history, dependencies, performance signals, and review feedback as a relational software graph. It combines Adaptive Evolutionary Code Reasoning (AECR) with a multi-agent design consisting of the Innovator, Critic, and Historian to generate, evaluate, and refine candidate repairs over multiple cycles rather than emitting a single patch. The framework further introduces EvoGraph Memory for persistent cross-release learning and Self-Reflexive Validation for explainable pre-merge verification. Experiments on repository-level repair benchmarks show that EvoGraphCoder improves patch quality and robustness over strong baselines, while maintaining positive improvement across repeated repair cycles. These results suggest that graph-driven evolutionary reasoning with persistent memory offers a practical path toward reliable and explainable AI-assisted software maintenance."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "cd121fa6f1d05818349ce73c7c28f47b35b5abad", "externalIds": {"DBLP": "journals/access/LeeKLKJKL26", "DOI": "10.1109/ACCESS.2026.3684055", "CorpusId": 287580159}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/cd121fa6f1d05818349ce73c7c28f47b35b5abad", "title": "A Hierarchical LLM-Based Framework for Heterogeneous Multi-Robot Orchestration in High-Risk Energy Facility Maintenance", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "https://doi.org/10.1109/access.2026.3684055", "status": "GOLD", "license": "CCBY", "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://api.unpaywall.org/v2/10.1109/ACCESS.2026.3684055?email=<INSERT_YOUR_EMAIL> or https://doi.org/10.1109/ACCESS.2026.3684055, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": null, "authors": [{"authorId": "28103312", "name": "Jungi Lee"}, {"authorId": "2280101409", "name": "Seu-jan Kim"}, {"authorId": "1830392471", "name": "Geonhyup Lee"}, {"authorId": "2269762328", "name": "Kangmin Kim"}, {"authorId": "2285032059", "name": "Jimin Jeon"}, {"authorId": "2856823", "name": "S. Ko"}, {"authorId": "2357453806", "name": "Kyoobin Lee"}], "abstract": "Maintenance and operation of critical energy infrastructure require extreme precision and strict adherence to safety protocols to prevent catastrophic failures. While recent advancements in general-purpose Vision-Language-Action (VLA) foundation models have shown promise in robotics, their inherent stochasticity and lack of procedural precision often result in unacceptable safety risks in high-stakes industrial environments. To address these limitations, this paper proposes a novel Hierarchical Multi-Robot Orchestration Framework designed for the coordinated control of heterogeneous robots in energy facility maintenance. The proposed framework decouples high-level cognitive reasoning from low-level execution by utilizing a local Large Language Model (LLM) as a strategic orchestrator. Grounded in a Structured Environmental Representation ( $\\mathcal {M}$ ), the LLM employs Chain-of-Thought (CoT) reasoning to decompose complex maintenance missions into verifiable sub-tasks, effectively bridging the gap between linguistic intent and physical constraints. These tasks are then dispatched to specialized execution modules: a manipulation unit utilizing Action Chunking with Transformers (ACT) adapted for high-precision industrial tasks, an autonomous navigation unit for Simultaneous Localization and Mapping (SLAM)-based pathfinding, and a vision-guided logistics unit for retrieval. Experimental evaluations in a simulated power plant environment validate the efficacy of the framework. A comparative ablation study demonstrates that utilizing structured environmental grounding significantly enhances the reasoning reliability of large-scale models compared to unstructured text baselines. Specifically, the GPT-OSS (20B) model achieved a peak planning success rate of 91.7%, with a notable proficiency in handling long-horizon Strategic commands (73.3%), validating that structural clarity is essential for complex causal inference. Furthermore, the integrated execution layer demonstrated exceptional reliability, achieving a 90% success rate specifically in distribution panel maintenance tasks, confirming that the hierarchical decoupling of probabilistic reasoning and deterministic execution provides a reliable solution for autonomous maintenance."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "632d22249ed391f006b31f9ed8cd762818c62cda", "externalIds": {"DBLP": "journals/access/YangLZ26a", "DOI": "10.1109/ACCESS.2026.3690228", "CorpusId": 288054008}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/632d22249ed391f006b31f9ed8cd762818c62cda", "title": "CraftGround: A High-Performance Reinforcement Learning Environment Based on Minecraft", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "https://doi.org/10.1109/access.2026.3690228", "status": "GOLD", "license": "CCBY", "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://api.unpaywall.org/v2/10.1109/ACCESS.2026.3690228?email=<INSERT_YOUR_EMAIL> or https://doi.org/10.1109/ACCESS.2026.3690228, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": null, "authors": [{"authorId": "2300252317", "name": "Hyeonseo Yang"}, {"authorId": "2152165744", "name": "Min-Su Lee"}, {"authorId": "1692756", "name": "Byoung-Tak Zhang"}], "abstract": "Recent trends in reinforcement learning have focused on developing agents that can operate in open-ended, multimodal environments and can be generalized to real-world tasks. Minecraft offers a rich, procedurally generated world that supports hierarchical skill learning, long-term planning, and complex decision-making, making it a compelling testbed for advancing generalizable reinforcement learning research. However, existing Minecraft-based frameworks such as Malm\u00f6, despite their early contributions, suffer from slow simulation speeds, outdated dependencies, and limited extensibility\u2013posing significant challenges for modern, large-scale reinforcement learning research. To address these limitations, we introduce CraftGround, a next-generation high-performance Minecraft-based reinforcement learning benchmark environment. Rather than simply improving upon Malm\u00f6, CraftGround is designed from the ground up for scalability, multimodal interaction, and experimental flexibility. It achieves up to $2.79\\times $ faster simulation performance and supports sound subtitles for richer perception. It also provides a unified action configuration that allows seamless switching between discrete and human-like controls without reconfiguration. These advancements make CraftGround a robust and future-ready platform for complex and realistic reinforcement learning research. We further assess its usability through two RL experiments, showing that CraftGround supports practical learning in representative settings. The environment is open-sourced under the GNU General Public License v3.0 and publicly available at https://github.com/yhs0602/CraftGround"}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "8fafa9710667012df70650033f93213097a7a0aa", "externalIds": {"DBLP": "journals/tmlr/FangYZYLLLL26", "CorpusId": 288674116}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/8fafa9710667012df70650033f93213097a7a0aa", "title": "EgoPlan: Towards Effective Embodied Agents via Egocentric Planning", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": null, "authors": [{"authorId": "2315927688", "name": "Zhi-Rui Fang"}, {"authorId": "2267843422", "name": "Ming Yang"}, {"authorId": "2315923491", "name": "Wei-Shuai Zeng"}, {"authorId": "2290003920", "name": "Jun-Peng Yue"}, {"authorId": "2290141409", "name": "Boyu Li"}, {"authorId": "2008151131", "name": "Jiafei Lyu"}, {"authorId": "2302950538", "name": "Xiu Li"}, {"authorId": "2258676670", "name": "Zong-Qing Lu"}], "abstract": null}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "90366dcd54a986699714668567560bb3ebe5d8b1", "externalIds": {"DBLP": "journals/access/AssidiqiAAC26", "DOI": "10.1109/ACCESS.2026.3696096", "CorpusId": 288639301}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/90366dcd54a986699714668567560bb3ebe5d8b1", "title": "Benchmarking Reference-Free LLM Agent Robustness Under Schema, Policy, and Toolset Drift", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "https://doi.org/10.1109/access.2026.3696096", "status": "GOLD", "license": "CCBY", "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://api.unpaywall.org/v2/10.1109/ACCESS.2026.3696096?email=<INSERT_YOUR_EMAIL> or https://doi.org/10.1109/ACCESS.2026.3696096, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": null, "authors": [{"authorId": "122720780", "name": "Moh Hasbi Assidiqi"}, {"authorId": "2294495148", "name": "Daniyal M. Alghazzawi"}, {"authorId": "2510081", "name": "Suaad S. Alarifi"}, {"authorId": "84739230", "name": "Li Cheng"}], "abstract": "Tool-using large language model (LLM) agents fail when external interfaces evolve, yet most evaluations emphasize static competence rather than adaptation under drift. We studied this problem with controlled schema, policy, and toolset perturbations derived from tau2-bench retail tasks and distinguished between a reference-guided replay benchmark and reference-free adaptation. In a reference-guided exact-trajectory setting, GPT-4o mini reached 0.708 task success over 216 trials, establishing that the benchmark contains recoverable structure. We then evaluated five OpenRouter-served models\u2014GPT-4o mini, GPT-4o, Kimi K2, DeepSeek V3, and Qwen3-32B\u2014under a reference-free protocol scoring end-state task completion across 270 trials per model-condition cell (30 scenarios $\\times 3$ episodes $\\times 3$ drift families). Static baselines range from 0.130 to 0.432 across models. Blind replay consistently degrades reference-free task success relative to static for four of five models; Kimi K2 is the exception, for which both blind replay and rules-only significantly improve over the static baseline. Rules-only shows model-dependent effects, significantly improving for Kimi K2 and Qwen3-32B while showing no significant difference for the remaining three models. A normalization-ablation condition confirms that the drift-inversion layer is load-bearing for toolset drift; schema-obedience determines whether this layer is effective. Within the three evaluated drift types and controller conditions, the reference-guided replay advantage does not transfer to reference-free task success, and the primary barriers are hidden-state reconstruction and branch selection across multiple candidate orders and products."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "98b395802c2d7e6eda2e0f4796f358a5652b7faa", "externalIds": {"DBLP": "journals/corr/abs-2603-29002", "DOI": "10.48550/arXiv.2603.29002", "CorpusId": 288855326}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/98b395802c2d7e6eda2e0f4796f358a5652b7faa", "title": "Understand and Accelerate Memory Processing Pipeline for Disaggregated LLM Inference", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: The following paper fields have been elided by the publisher: {'abstract'}. Paper or abstract available at https://api.unpaywall.org/v2/10.48550/arXiv.2603.29002?email=<INSERT_YOUR_EMAIL> or https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2603.29002, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": null, "authors": [{"authorId": "2294936058", "name": "Zifan He"}, {"authorId": "2387892681", "name": "Rui Ma"}, {"authorId": "2266846937", "name": "Yi-Zhou Sun"}, {"authorId": "2257233300", "name": "Jason Cong"}], "abstract": null}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "e8b9771bdb2dcf4f2adc8b63fad429eeac7866e0", "externalIds": {"DBLP": "journals/access/NechesovR26", "DOI": "10.1109/ACCESS.2026.3697396", "CorpusId": 288752579}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/e8b9771bdb2dcf4f2adc8b63fad429eeac7866e0", "title": "The AGI-Powered Metaverse: A Decentralized Task-Based Cognitive Architecture", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "https://doi.org/10.1109/access.2026.3697396", "status": "GOLD", "license": "CCBY", "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://api.unpaywall.org/v2/10.1109/ACCESS.2026.3697396?email=<INSERT_YOUR_EMAIL> or https://doi.org/10.1109/ACCESS.2026.3697396, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": null, "authors": [{"authorId": "87670088", "name": "A. Nechesov"}, {"authorId": "2337026927", "name": "Janne Ruponen"}], "abstract": "The pursuit of Strong Artificial Intelligence (AGI) is hindered by the fundamental limitations of Large Language Models (LLMs), such as hallucinations, static knowledge, and a lack of verifiable reasoning. This paper proposes a hybrid, decentralized cognitive architecture for an AGI-powered Metaverse that addresses these challenges. The framework with Task-Based Cognitive Architecture (TBCA) establishes a closed cognitive loop integrating three core components: an LLM for creative hypothesis generation (\u201cSystem 1\u201d), a symbolic logic engine for rigorous verification (\u201cSystem 2\u201d), and a decentralized multi-blockchain knowledge base serving as a secure long-term memory for collective intelligence. We demonstrate within a simulated Metaverse environment that this architecture supports cumulative learning and solves complex tasks, such as legal reasoning, which are intractable for isolated LLMs. The key innovations include a probabilistic knowledge hierarchy for explainable reasoning and a multi-blockchain structure that ensures tamper-proof knowledge sharing among multiple AI agents. This approach provides a foundational framework for verifiable and cumulative learning in dynamic multi-agent environments, paving the way for transparent, robust, and continuously evolving AI systems within the Metaverse."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "291dab61be7b2170eb1bcf7ecec5557373ff0f49", "externalIds": {"DBLP": "journals/corr/abs-2606-04111", "DOI": "10.48550/arXiv.2606.04111", "CorpusId": 288940341}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/291dab61be7b2170eb1bcf7ecec5557373ff0f49", "title": "AgenticDiffusion: Agentic Diffusion-based Path Planning for Vision-Based UAV Navigation", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: The following paper fields have been elided by the publisher: {'abstract'}. Paper or abstract available at https://api.unpaywall.org/v2/10.48550/arXiv.2606.04111?email=<INSERT_YOUR_EMAIL> or https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2606.04111, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": null, "authors": [{"authorId": "2345607981", "name": "Faryal Batool"}, {"authorId": "2311376998", "name": "Muhammad Ahsan Mustafa"}, {"authorId": "2229366964", "name": "Fawad Mehboob"}, {"authorId": "2047999738", "name": "Valerii Serpiva"}, {"authorId": "48470616", "name": "D. Tsetserukou"}], "abstract": null}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "9f0a7d3cdc3458fecaf1e4062fdb9e2d4dff37fe", "externalIds": {"DBLP": "journals/corr/abs-2604-15719", "DOI": "10.48550/arXiv.2604.15719", "CorpusId": 289311232}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/9f0a7d3cdc3458fecaf1e4062fdb9e2d4dff37fe", "title": "The World Leaks the Future: Harness Evolution for Future Prediction Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: The following paper fields have been elided by the publisher: {'abstract'}. Paper or abstract available at https://api.unpaywall.org/v2/10.48550/arXiv.2604.15719?email=<INSERT_YOUR_EMAIL> or https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2604.15719, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": null, "authors": [{"authorId": "2332884465", "name": "Chuyang Wei"}, {"authorId": "2147417083", "name": "Mao Gao"}, {"authorId": "2306627722", "name": "Zhi-Xin Han"}, {"authorId": "2398377257", "name": "Kefei Chen"}